WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.160
The first film built a rule.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.120
Rain falls where moist air rises, and warmer months need more rain to count as

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.120
humid.

4
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.753
Now test it on two made-up stations.

5
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:17.933
Both average about 26 °C.

6
00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:20.353
What grows at each?

7
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:23.767
Station A is the one you just worked.

8
00:00:25.027 --> 00:00:28.247
Its blue rain line rides above the red in every month.

9
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:35.870
No month is dry. Its class is Af: tropical, with no dry month.

10
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.990
That is a tropical rainforest climate.

11
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.640
Station B has the same average warmth, though its months run from about 24 to 30 °C.

12
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.560
But it gets about two thirds as much rain, and most of it falls from April to

13
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.560
October.

14
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.560
From November to March, the blue line drops below the red.

15
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.400
Those five months fill with gold.

16
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.740
They are the dry season.

17
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.600
Its class is Aw: tropical, with a marked dry season.

18
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.780
That is a tropical savanna climate.

19
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.820
The rule's first guess is grass with scattered trees.

20
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.170
So far the rule gives two first guesses.

21
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.570
Similar warmth, different rain, different climate classes.

22
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.990
Now look at station B's rain once more.

23
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:32.683
Staver, Archibald and Levin analysed tree cover across the tropics.

24
00:01:33.863 --> 00:01:38.703
They looked at places with about 1,000 to 2 ,000 and a half -thousand millimeters of

25
00:01:38.703 --> 00:01:39.703
rain a year.

26
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:45.733
Those places also had a dry season shorter than seven months, by the study's own

27
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:49.593
measure. There, tree cover was rarely in between.

28
00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:53.613
Places were mostly savanna, or mostly forest.

29
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:59.997
Station B's rain total falls in that range, and its diagram points to savanna.

30
00:02:01.257 --> 00:02:03.837
Would every place with a climate like that be savanna?

31
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:16.610
No. In that window, savanna and forest were both found under similar climates.

32
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.970
And fire was associated with keeping areas open.

33
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:24.733
Where grass is open, fire can keep it open.

34
00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:28.093
A closed canopy can hold fire back.

35
00:02:29.253 --> 00:02:31.193
So each state may help keep itself going.

36
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:35.023
This is a pattern, not proof.

37
00:02:36.643 --> 00:02:40.163
Savanna and forest may be alternative states under similar climates.

38
00:02:41.403 --> 00:02:44.383
Whether those states are truly stable is still debated.

39
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:47.997
So climate gives a first guess at the biome.

40
00:02:49.297 --> 00:02:53.437
Fire, soils, the timing of rain and microclimate can change the answer.

41
00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:57.437
A savanna climate does not guarantee a savanna.
